, "model": {"prior_margin": 6.01, "prior_sd": 5.34, "poll_margin": 9.06, "poll_sd": 5.63, "poll_weight": 0.47, "fundamentals": {"margin": 7.24, "lean": -1.5, "environment": 4.15, "incumbency": 4.0, "money": 0.59}, "rating_margin": 5.19, "sigma_race": 5.2, "sigma_total": 5.62, "baseline_margin": 7.46, "analyst_adjustment": -0.66, "key_factors": ["October polls uniformly show Hobbs ahead by 4-17 points, including Republican-sponsored surveys", "Biggs's polarizing Trump-aligned, election-denier profile in a state Hobbs carried in 2022", "Hobbs's roughly 3-to-1 fundraising edge and heavy spending advantage", "Prediction markets price Hobbs near 90 percent despite Arizona's slight Republican lean"], "rationale": "Every recent poll, including Republican-sponsored surveys, shows Hobbs leading, and markets price her near ninety percent while raters call the race Lean to Likely Democratic. Her fundraising edge and Biggs's polarizing record outweigh Arizona's Republican lean, though thin effective polling and the state's history of narrow margins argue for caution.", "watch": "A late shift of Latino or moderate voters toward Biggs, or a 2022-style polling miss, would move this toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Katie Hobbs (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's governor's race pits incumbent Democrat Katie Hobbs against Republican Congressman Andy Biggs, with minor-party candidates also on the ballot. Polling through early October consistently shows Hobbs ahead, with leads ranging from four to seventeen points, including surveys funded by Republican sponsors. Aggregators place her advantage between five and eleven points. Prediction markets give Hobbs roughly a ninety percent chance of winning, and most race raters call the contest Lean Democratic, with one calling it Likely Democratic.\n\nHobbs holds a large financial edge, having raised about three times Biggs's total, and headlines note she is outspending him by millions. Biggs's profile as a